WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.000
A growth cone meets many bundles of axons.

2
00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:08.340
Its filopodia touch several.

3
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:10.340
Why does it join just one?

4
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:14.580
When axons grow bundled together, they are fasciculated.

5
00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:17.720
When one leaves its bundle, it defasciculates.

6
00:00:20.233 --> 00:00:25.333
In grasshopper embryos, Raper, Bastiani and Goodman followed a neuron called G.

7
00:00:26.317 --> 00:00:31.257
Its growth cone joins one bundle, the A/P fascicle, made of axons from neurons

8
00:00:31.257 --> 00:00:34.857
called A and P. It grows along the P axons.

9
00:00:35.817 --> 00:00:39.957
Before G arrived, the team killed the P neurons, the neurons, or both.

10
00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.570
If any bundle would do, G should take a neighbour.

11
00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:45.530
What did G do?

12
00:00:51.233 --> 00:00:55.173
Without the P axons, G failed to extend far along its route.

13
00:00:56.033 --> 00:00:59.033
It wandered, branched abnormally, and took no other bundle.

14
00:01:00.067 --> 00:01:03.087
Without only the axons, G behaved normally.

15
00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:11.710
G needs something particular to the P axons.

16
00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:17.750
Hence the labeled pathway hypothesis: bundles carry surface labels that growth cones

17
00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:18.750
recognize.

18
00:01:19.333 --> 00:01:24.273
In the fruit fly, the search found adhesion molecules, not a unique label for each

19
00:01:24.273 --> 00:01:25.273
bundle.

20
00:01:25.583 --> 00:01:29.583
Fasciclin II can bind Fasciclin II on a neighbouring axon.

21
00:01:30.403 --> 00:01:34.103
Without it, axons that travel together in certain pathways drift apart.

22
00:01:37.217 --> 00:01:42.617
In a separate experiment, extra Fasciclin II kept motor axons stuck in their nerve.

23
00:01:43.377 --> 00:01:44.537
They bypassed their muscles.

24
00:01:45.617 --> 00:01:47.857
Opposite changes, opposite results.

25
00:01:48.777 --> 00:01:51.277
The amount of adhesion between axons matters.

26
00:01:52.367 --> 00:01:56.127
A second gene, beaten path, makes a protein on motor axons.

27
00:01:57.007 --> 00:01:59.527
Without it, motor axons fail to leave their nerve.

28
00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:03.540
Now reduce Fasciclin II in a beaten path mutant.

29
00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:05.640
What happens at the choice point?

30
00:02:11.633 --> 00:02:13.493
The bypass defect is suppressed.

31
00:02:14.653 --> 00:02:17.013
Motor axons can often leave their nerve again.

32
00:02:19.117 --> 00:02:21.537
Lowering adhesion makes up for losing Beat.

33
00:02:22.477 --> 00:02:25.857
So Beat works at least partly against adhesion between axons.

34
00:02:26.867 --> 00:02:31.647
Beat also helps motor axons recognize a pathway cue called Sidestep.

35
00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.990
Last, the same cue can mean different things.

36
00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:41.210
In a simple comparison, netrin-1 can attract growth cones carrying the receptor DCC.

37
00:02:42.333 --> 00:02:47.533
With a second receptor, UNC5, beside DCC, it can repel them instead.

38
00:02:50.283 --> 00:02:54.743
The growth cone's internal state, and the surface beneath it, can also flip the

39
00:02:54.743 --> 00:02:55.743
response.

40
00:02:57.983 --> 00:02:59.263
A cue is a message.

41
00:03:00.183 --> 00:03:03.923
The receptors and internal state of the growth cone decide what it means.

42
00:03:06.067 --> 00:03:08.487
So followers choose which axons to ride.

43
00:03:09.287 --> 00:03:11.127
And the reader decides what a cue means.
